  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A much drier air mass arrives today as a cold front \npushes offshore. Expect windy conditions this afternoon behind the \nfront. \n\nCold front quickly pushes offshore early this morning. It will be \nmoisture starved with a very dry antecedent air mass. Will see \nincreasing winds and falling dewpoints this afternoon as the \nboundary layer becomes well-mixed. The combination of falling \nrelative humidity values (>40%) and gusty winds between 25 and 35 \nmph will pair to increase the fire risk. While we typically look for \nRH values at 35 percent and below, the prolonged dry stretch and \nleaf drop prior to today's elevated wind along with input from state \nforestry and fire weather partners has prompted fire weather SPS's \nfor the entire region. In terms of temperatures, expect a warmer day \nalong the I-95 corridor with highs near 70 degrees. Further inland, \nlocations will see cooler temps as diabatic warming competes with \nmoderate to strong cold air advection.  A sustained pretty tight \npressure gradient continues into Friday evening, which casts some \nuncertainty on low temperatures. We opted to keep temps from the mid \n30s to the low/mid 40s, highest towards the RI/eastern MA coasts \nwhere winds will take longer to slacken off, and coolest readings \ninland. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Yet more high pressure arrives this weekend into \nmuch of next week with a moderating temperature trend. The next \nbonafide chance for widespread precipitation arrives later next \nweek. \n\nA series of high pressures will dominate the weather pattern over \nsouthern New England this weekend clear through much of next week. \nThe post frontal airmass kicks off the weekend much cooler as 925 mb \ntemps drop from 12-15C Friday to 0-3C Saturday. Under dry NW flow \nbeneath a high building in from New York we'll see high temperatures \nclimb just into the mid to upper 50s on Halloween with evening temps \nin the 40s. Fortunately, by then the pressure gradient should have \nlessened enough that winds will be light. The light winds and clear \npost-frontal skies allow for radiational cooling Halloween night \nwith lows in the 20s inland and 30s across the coastal plain. \nSimilar weather picture on Sunday albeit a bit warmer as a weak \nsouthwesterly return flow develops as the center of high pressure \nmoves offshore. \n\nA mid-level trough looks to pass well to our northeast around \nTuesday but lack of moisture and the transient nature of the feature \nwill likely keep rain away before another high pressure takes hold \nWednesday into Thursday keeping dry and warmer than average weather \nin place until the pattern may break down moving into next weekend. \nGuidance is still largely split on the eventual evolution of the \nupper level pattern with a wide range of solutions possible. The \nmost aggressive guidance wrt to a pattern change is the GFS which \ndepicts a deep shortwave trough over the eastern CONUS by Thursday.",
